Identity-to-Topology Binding under Compromised Host Observation: 

A Formal Framework for Platform-Scoped Positional Attestation

ouadi Maakoul and SI TEAM 

Abstract

Cryptographic attestation establishes that a key belongs to a component and that the component's firmware matches a reference manifest. It does not establish where the component is. This dissertation develops a formal framework for determining which positional claims the attestation stack can express, which it can derive under stated assumptions, and which it cannot attain without anchoring that the verifier must supply.

The framework separates three position spaces — logical role P_1, bus-level topological reachability P_2, and physical slot P_3 — and orders attestation claims in a strict ladder L_0 \prec L_1 \prec L_2 \prec L_3 \prec L_4, in which SPDM establishes L_0 and L_1, TDISP establishes L_2, and claims over P_2 and P_3 require a positional mechanism whose existence and semantics are platform-dependent. It separates two adversary capabilities that the literature routinely conflates — a reporting-integrity adversary \mathbb{A}, a relay adversary \mathbb{B}_{\mathrm{rel}}, and a channel-integrity adversary \mathbb{B}_{\mathrm{int}} grounded in the specification-level weaknesses of PCIe IDE disclosed in December 2025 — and shows that \mathbb{B}_{\mathrm{rel}} and \mathbb{B}_{\mathrm{int}} are incomparable, so that no scalar measure of attack resistance is well defined. It replaces the path-disjointness heuristic for evidential independence with a dominator-based criterion, defines effective evidential support as a min-cut over adversary-admissible dominating sets, and compares candidate methods under a trust-assumption preorder that admits incomparability as a legitimate verdict rather than forcing a ranking.

The principal results are stated with their exact status. A host-mediated impossibility theorem holds under an explicit indistinguishability premise that host-mediation alone does not supply; where device-unique identity is checked against enrollment, the impossibility takes a different form, and the residual attacks are positional rather than identitary. The escape families that recover soundness in the residual stratum are a partition of the considered space, not a proof of exhaustiveness: distance and timing bounds are excluded intra-chassis on resolution grounds, and enrollment anchoring is a terminal event rather than an online observation. A stratum in which the channel-integrity predicate fails yields indeterminacy and forces abstention, not a demonstrated soundness failure.

The framework is falsification-oriented. Its most likely honest outcome, applied to a specific platform, is that the stratum closes — either because the available mechanisms already suffice, or because no mechanism can suffice without an assumption the verifier will not make. Both are admissible results.

