Appendix A — Consolidated Ledger of Validated Propositions and Technical Corrections

A.1 The TDISP Baseline

A.1.1. TDISP establishes an L_2 claim, not an L_4 claim. The guarantee is over the triple (\tau, \nu, \ell) where \tau is a TEE Device Interface, \nu a trusted VM, and \ell the protected link. The domain of the guarantee contains no term ranging over the position space \mathcal{P}.

Evidentiary basis. The TDISP architecture defines its primary focus as establishing trust between a TVM and a TDISP-capable device, securing the PCIe path, and attaching or detaching a TDI to or from a TVM. The TDI is an assignable device interface, for instance a virtual function. The DEVICE_INTERFACE_REPORT provides “a trusted report of the TDI configurations, the list of MMIO resources associated with the TDI, and the order in which they must be mapped into the TVM address space”, together with a nonce generated on transition to \texttt{CONFIG\_LOCKED}. It contains no field denoting the Root Port or the bus-device-function. Where a BDF appears in a device-signed artifact, the BDF is assigned by host enumeration, not by the device's hardware root.

Formal statement. Proposition 2 (§5.1 of the chapter) holds normatively, not merely definitionally.

A.1.2. B_4 must be stratified into B_{4\text{-}P} (mitigations applied and validated) and B_{4\text{-}U} (unpatched or unknown). B_{4\text{-}U} is a positive control, not a secure comparator.

Evidentiary basis. The PCI-SIG vulnerability note PCISIGVRT0001 (2025-12-09) states that all three vulnerabilities “potentially expose systems implementing IDE and TDISP, to an adversary that can breach isolation between trusted execution environments”. Because the conditions are specification-level and the mitigations are heterogeneous, a platform cannot be classified as secure merely because a patch is reported as applied.

A.2 The IDE Mitigation Space

A.2.1. The mitigation vector is heterogeneous: m_{9612} is a pair property of the link, m_{9613} is currently \mathsf{U} on all known shipping platforms, and m_{9614} is a per-component implementation property.

Evidentiary basis — m_{9612}. Intel states that IDE Escort “can only be enabled when the host processor and its partner device both support the feature”. The PCI-SIG Draft ECN “IDE TLP Reordering Enhancements” (2026-03-19) introduces IDE Escort as “an optional security enhancement mechanism intended to improve detection of forbidden IDE TLP reordering” and defines “additional IDE ordering enforcement rules required by IDE Escort”. It is therefore a property of the pair, not of a single component.

Evidentiary basis — m_{9613}. Intel states that “the current TDISP specification does not define a policy to securely handle this scenario”, that “CTR can affect all compliant platforms, including Intel products”, and that “future Intel processors may adopt mitigations recommended by the revised TDISP specification”. No deployable mitigation exists on any shipping platform; m_{9613} = \mathsf{U} is the correct value everywhere.

Evidentiary basis — m_{9614}. The Delayed Posted Redirection vulnerability arises “when a security module fails to flush or re-key an IDE stream before rebinding a PCIe device to a new Trusted Domain Interface”. Mitigation is an implementation-level flush or re-key discipline at rebinding, validated per firmware version.

A.2.2. \rho(\ell) = 1 (programmable switch in the link path) is effectively disqualifying on current vendor guidance.

Evidentiary basis. Intel recommends that “programmable PCIe switches should not be used in Intel TDX Connect and IDE deployments that involve affected processors” for all three vulnerabilities. The disjunct \mathsf{validated\_mitigation}_{\mathrm{switch}}(\ell) in the formula of §6.3 is therefore nearly empty in practice.

A.2.3. The channel-integrity predicate \mathcal{I}(\ell, [t_0, t_1]) = \mathcal{I}_{\mathrm{ord}} \wedge \mathcal{I}_{\mathrm{tag}} \wedge \mathcal{I}_{\mathrm{flush}} is necessary but not sufficient for positional soundness. Its failure yields indeterminacy, not a demonstrated soundness failure.

Evidentiary basis. The IDE flaws affect data consumed on the link — MMIO reads, DMA writes, and posted writes — while the TDISP state is a distinct object. Proposition 4 (§6.4) establishes that if \mathcal{I}^{*}(\ell) fails, the verifier's TDISP-state observation is consistent with at least two link histories, one satisfying and one violating the positional invariant. It does not establish that the verifier accepts a false positional claim. Abstention is the sound response.

A.3 The PPCIE Baseline

A.3.1. The PPCIE verifier version must be recorded. Python 1.x is deprecated; C++ 2.x is current.

Evidentiary basis. NVIDIA's migration guide states that “the Python Attestation SDK is deprecated as of March 15, 2026”, with “end of support: September 15, 2026”, and directs users of nv-ppcie-verifier v1.x to migrate to nv-ppcie-verifier v2.x (ppcie-verifier-sdk-cpp), which uses nvattest internally instead of the Python SDK.

A.3.2. PPCIE topology check inputs are not specified in public documentation; “driver-enumerated” is an unproven claim. The provenance of the check's inputs must be treated as unknown and tested in scenario S_2.

A.3.3. The PDI claim's semantics — signer, scope, freshness — are not in the public claim schema. B_{3b} is “to be tested”, not “presumed positional”.

A.4 The Unprivileged Topology Certificate

A.4.1. B_5 is a fingerprint and coarse-location comparator, not a slot baseline. It supports Z^{\mathrm{cont}} (continuity) but not Z^{\mathrm{pos}} (positional attribution).

Evidentiary basis. The arXiv:2606.24934 scope statement targets per-SM timing fingerprint, hardware class, and coarse network location, not physical slot occupation.

A.5 Device Identity

A.5.1. NVIDIA device attestation uses device-unique ECC-384 (ECDSA P-384) keys, with a certificate chain anchored in an NVIDIA CA.

Evidentiary basis. NVIDIA documentation lists SHA-384 and ECC_NIST_P384 as the algorithms for SPDM-based device attestation on BlueField-3 and ConnectX-7. The consequence is structural: two GPUs with identical firmware and reference manifests produce distinguishable signatures at the identity level.

A.5.2. The identity-distinguished relay case has a different structure from the indistinguishability case. Where device-unique identity is checked against enrollment, a relay to a different device produces a detectable identity mismatch. The residual attacks are relay of the same device across ports and falsification of port labels by the compromised host.

Formal statement. Proposition 7 (§9.3 of the chapter).

A.6 The Impossibility Result

A.6.1. Theorem 1 requires an indistinguishability premise: the honest and relayed worlds must induce identical distributions on \mathsf{View}_V. Host-mediation alone does not imply this premise, because the relayed-to component signs with its own key.

A.6.2. Without the premise, Theorem 1 is false for any verifier that checks device identity against enrollment. The correct formulation is the conditional form in §9.2.

A.7 The Escape-Family Partition

A.7.1. Theorem 2 is a partition of the considered families, not a proof of exhaustiveness.

A.7.2. Distance and timing bounds are excluded intra-chassis on resolution grounds. The relevant delay differences are on the order of nanoseconds (backplane propagation), while documented distance-bounding mechanisms for hardware attestation operate at resolutions of order 10^{-4} seconds.

A.7.3. Enrollment anchoring is a terminal event of Proposition 3, not an online observation of Theorem 2. The provenance graph must separate V_{\mathrm{enr}} from V_{\mathrm{obs}}.

A.8 The Residual-Stratum Impossibility

A.8.1. The dstack security model acknowledges the residual gap directly: “dstack also cannot rule out a live relay/cuckoo attack to a genuine remote GPU: current Hopper/Blackwell deployments do not provide a CPU-TEE-verifiable TEE-I/O/TDISP device binding”. A GPU attestation result “establishes that a genuine CC-enabled GPU is reachable and responsive now, not that the device is bound to this TD”.

This is deployed-system evidence that the residual stratum is real and configuration-dependent. It establishes relevance, not novelty.

