Appendix B — Evidentiary Basis for Each Correction

B.1 On the TDISP Interface Report

The DEVICE_INTERFACE_REPORT provides a trusted report of the TDI configurations, the list of MMIO resources associated with the TDI, and the order in which they must be mapped into the TVM address space. A nonce is generated by the device on transition to \texttt{CONFIG\_LOCKED} and verified on transition to \texttt{RUN}, providing the property that all transitions through the TDISP state machine occur due to requests generated in the same SPDM secure session. No field in the report denotes the Root Port or the BDF.

B.2 On the IDE Specification Vulnerabilities

The PCI-SIG vulnerability note describes three distinct specification-level weaknesses.

CVE-2025-9612 (Forbidden IDE Reordering). A weakness in TLP ordering enforcement allows MITM attackers to reorder IDE-protected packets undetected, violating data integrity protections. Intel describes the mechanism: IDE Sync messages are sent rarely (at most 0.4\% of IDE traffic), and after an IDE Sync is sent, a malicious switch can reorder a subsequent NPR or Completion to precede the Sync, preventing immediate detection.

CVE-2025-9613 (Completion Timeout Redirection). When an IDE-protected NPR times out, its tag may be released and reused by a subsequent request. A delayed completion of the original request arriving after the new request causes the receiver to consume stale or incorrect data, violating both integrity and confidentiality objectives.

CVE-2025-9614 (Delayed Posted Redirection). A security module that fails to flush or re-key an IDE stream before rebinding a PCIe device to a new TDI can deliver stale posted writes from the previous context to the new one, violating confidentiality or security objectives.

B.3 On the Absence of Public Proofs-of-Concept

Vulnerability databases consulted during this work report no public proof-of-concept for CVE-2025-9612. This is consistent with the controlled conditions described by Intel and with the requirement in the specification-level mitigations for particular hardware combinations. The consequence for the protocol is that S_7-C (no reproduction possible, configuration and mitigation evidence documented) must be an admissible outcome, with the vulnerability marked as an unresolved security assumption.

B.4 On the PPCIE Verifier Version

The migration is explicit: nv-ppcie-verifier v1.x is the Python SDK-based verifier and is deprecated; nv-ppcie-verifier v2.x is the C++ implementation using nvattest. A baseline description that omits the version conflates two implementations with different dependency stacks and different support status.

B.5 On the dstack Security Model

The dstack model states that the boot event “does not prove that the same device is still attached”, that a malicious host may attempt PCI hot-remove or replacement, and that current Hopper/Blackwell deployments do not provide a CPU-TEE-verifiable TEE-I/O/TDISP device binding. A GPU report “establishes that a genuine CC-enabled GPU is reachable and responsive now, not that the device is bound to this TD”. This is the deployed-system acknowledgment that the residual stratum is not universally closed.

