Appendix E — Formal Results and Their Exact Status

E.1 Theorem 1 (Host-Mediated Impossibility, Indistinguishability Form)

Statement. Let V be a verifier all of whose observation functions are host-mediated, and let \kappa \succeq \mathbb{B}_{\mathrm{rel}}. Let c, c' be components such that for every o_j \in O, the distributions of o_j in the honest world W_0 (with \beta(c) = p) and the relayed world W_1 (with \beta(c') = p' \neq p and the session relayed to p) are identical. Then V is not \kappa-sound for L_3 or L_4 with error \varepsilon < 1.

Proof. Since views are identically distributed and \alpha is a function of the view, \Pr[\alpha(\mathsf{View}_V) = 1 \mid W_0] = \Pr[\alpha(\mathsf{View}_V) = 1 \mid W_1]. Completeness in W_0 requires the left side to be at least 1 - \delta; hence the right side is at least 1 - \delta. Since \beta(c) \neq p in W_1, soundness requires the right side to be at most \varepsilon, so \varepsilon \ge 1 - \delta. \square

Status. Conditional result. The indistinguishability premise is not a consequence of host-mediation. It is an additional condition on the pair (c, c') and on the verifier's predicate. When the premise fails — as it does whenever the verifier checks device-unique identity against enrollment — the theorem does not apply, and the identity-distinguished case of Proposition 7 governs.

E.2 Proposition 7 (Identity-Distinguished Relay)

Statement. Suppose the verifier checks device-unique identity against an enrollment record, and c, c' have distinct device certificates. A relay to c' then produces an identity mismatch the verifier can detect. However, this does not establish positional soundness. Two residual attacks remain: (a) relay of c itself to a different port, and (b) falsification of the port label by the compromised host. In both cases the positional component of the claim is host-supplied and is not protected by the identity check.

Status. Unconditional for verifiers that check device-unique identity, given the evidentiary basis in Appendix A.5.1. The consequence for S_{3c} is that the experiment must specify whether device-unique identity is in or out of the verifier's view. If in, the experiment tests L_3 soundness under identity-distinguished conditions. If out, it tests the weaker indistinguishability case. Both are informative; they are not the same test.

E.3 Proposition 2 (TDISP Scope)

Statement. TDISP establishes L_2. It establishes L_3 if and only if an independently trusted map from Root Port to TDI is available and validated. It establishes L_4 if and only if, in addition, \sigma is trusted.

Status. Retained, with the normative basis in Appendix B.1. The DEVICE_INTERFACE_REPORT contains no port-denoting field; a BDF in a device-signed artifact is host-assigned.

E.4 Proposition 3 (Anchoring Termination)

Statement. Let \kappa be an adversary class, and suppose that every terminal event not trusted by the verifier is assumable by some adversary in \kappa. Then every \kappa-sound positional claim possesses a finite anchoring chain whose terminal event is trusted by the verifier.

Status. Retained, with the premise stated explicitly. The derivation relation is required to be well-founded. Without the premise, the proposition is close to a tautology.

E.5 Proposition 4 (Stratum Indeterminacy)

Statement. If \mathcal{I}^{*}(\ell) fails during the observation interval, the verifier's observation of the TDISP state is consistent with at least two link histories, one in which the positional invariant holds and one in which it does not, and the verifier cannot distinguish them from the observation alone. The correct response is abstention.

Status. Deliberately weaker than a soundness statement. It establishes indeterminacy, not a demonstrated soundness failure. A soundness failure would require constructing two link histories with the same TDISP state and showing indistinguishability to the verifier's predicate. The CVE descriptions alone do not supply that construction. The weaker claim is sufficient, because indeterminacy forces abstention under pre-registered rules.

E.6 Theorem 2 (Escape Families, Partition Form)

Statement. Let V be a verifier and \kappa \succeq \mathbb{B}_{\mathrm{rel}}. If V is \kappa-sound for L_3 in \Sigma_{\mathrm{res}} under the identity-distinguished condition of Proposition 7, then at least one of the following holds:

1. (Independent observation) V contains an observation function o whose provenance is not dominated by any element of \kappa acting on the observation path, and whose anchoring chain terminates in a trusted event.
2. (Channel binding) V verifies a signature over a channel identifier produced by a hardware root that is itself position-anchored, and the signature binds the endpoint, the channel, and the challenge nonce.
3. (Position-sensitive measurement) V verifies a measurement whose distribution differs across positions with non-negligible advantage for every adversary in \kappa.

Status. A partition of the considered families, not a proof of exhaustiveness. Two further families are excluded by separate argument: distance and timing bounds (excluded intra-chassis on resolution grounds) and enrollment anchoring (a terminal event, not an online observation).

E.7 Proposition 5 (Independence Is Not Path-Disjointness)

Statement. Path-disjointness in the provenance graph is necessary but not sufficient for \kappa-independence.

Status. Retained. Two paths may be edge-disjoint yet share a dominator: host enumeration and BMC inventory may both depend on the same PCIe switch; two signed claims may both depend on the same device key or the same enrollment event.

E.8 Proposition 6 (The Trust-Assumption Preorder)

Statement. \preceq_\kappa is a preorder. It is not total.

Status. Retained. Non-totality is exhibited by any pair that moves trust from one domain to another. A method that replaces a host observer by a BMC observer has A^{\mathrm{obs}} incomparable when the BMC is neither stronger nor weaker than the host for the relevant \kappa. The correct verdict is neither “improvement” nor “regression” but “incomparable”.

E.9 Corollary 1 (Composition Bound, Logical Form)

Statement. For a verifier that composes an L_3 claim with a slot map \sigma, if either the L_3 claim or \sigma fails, the composed L_4 claim fails. Hence \varepsilon_{P_3} \ge \max(\varepsilon_{L_3}, \varepsilon_\sigma), with no tightness claim.

Status. A logical claim about necessary conditions, not a probabilistic equality. The equality clause in earlier formulations required an independence model not supplied by the evidence.

