Appendix G — Methodological Choices That Are Not Empirical Claims

The following are design decisions. They are correct as choices, but they must not be presented as findings.

G.1 Provenance graph formalization. The separation of V_{\mathrm{claim}}, V_{\mathrm{obs}}, V_{\mathrm{root}}, V_{\mathrm{chan}}, V_{\mathrm{enr}} is a modeling choice. It does not assert that any particular platform's provenance structure has this form.

G.2 Effective support as min-cut. \mathrm{Supp}_\kappa(S) = \min_D |D| is a quantitative choice. It measures evidential redundancy against \kappa; it does not claim to capture every aspect of independence.

G.3 Trust-assumption preorder. \preceq_\kappa is a design choice for comparison. The specific lattices \mathcal{L}_k must be instantiated per platform.

G.4 Paired McNemar design. The exact/asymptotic split at n_d = 25 is a standard statistical choice. Pilot data must establish whether the discordance rate is compatible.

G.5 AURC as secondary endpoint. AURC is meaningful only when a score exists. The choice to make paired correctness primary reflects that many candidate methods will emit three-valued outputs without a score.

G.6 Three-level S_7 validation. Admitting S_7-C (no reproduction possible) as a valid outcome is a methodological decision reflecting the absence of public PoCs for the IDE CVEs and the difficulty of constructing a controlled reproduction without an affected implementation.

G.7 Ground-truth blinding and randomization. A protocol-integrity decision, not an empirical claim about what unblinded experiments would show.

G.8 Separation of Z^{\mathrm{pos}} and Z^{\mathrm{cont}}. A methodological decision reflecting that position and continuity are conceptually distinct capabilities. It does not assert that any candidate will differentiate them in practice.

