Appendix H — Reading Discipline

Three habits of reading are required of anyone applying this framework.

First, distinguish claim level from mechanism. A mechanism named B_4 does not establish an L_4 claim. A candidate named M_1-C does not establish a positional escape merely by being a channel-bound variant. Every claim must be read at the ladder level at which it is established, and no higher.

Second, distinguish possibility from capability. “The adversary can present the same view if the components are indistinguishable” is a possibility conditional on indistinguishability. “The adversary can present the same view” is a capability claim requiring the premise. The framework uses the conditional form and states the premise explicitly, because the unconditioned form is false for verifiers that check device-unique identity.

Third, distinguish indeterminacy from soundness failure. A stratum in which the verifier cannot exclude a violating history is a stratum in which the verifier must abstain. It is not, without an explicit construction, a stratum in which the verifier has been demonstrated to accept a false claim. The framework uses the weaker form because the weaker form is what the evidence supports, and because the weaker form is sufficient to force the correct operational response.

The framework's most likely honest outcome, applied to any specific platform, is that the stratum closes — either because the available mechanisms already suffice, or because no mechanism can suffice without an assumption the verifier will not make. Both are success conditions of the audit. The appendices record the technical content that makes this outcome decidable.

